}

{
  "task_summary": "Predict whether a taxi trip outcome of interest will occur from pre-trip context under distribution shift across environments.",
  "feature_id": 4,
  "score": 0.74,
  "rationale": "Time of day captures stable human mobility and traffic patterns that usually remain predictive across domains, but its effect can shift with local schedules, seasonality, and environment-specific demand cycles, so it is useful but not fully invariant."
}

{
  "task_summary": "Predict whether a taxi trip will have a positive tip amount from pre-trip and trip metadata under distribution shift.",
  "feature_id": 4,
  "score": 0.74,
  "rationale": "Time of day is plausibly and causally related to rider purpose, traffic, airport/nightlife demand, and tipping behavior, so it should retain moderate predictive value across domains; however, exact clock time can also reflect city-specific schedules, policy changes, and temporal sampling shifts, making it less stable than core trip attributes."
}

{
  "task_summary": "Predict whether a taxi trip outcome on WhyShift's taxi task will occur using pre-trip and trip-context information under distribution shift.",
  "feature_id": 4,
  "score": 0.78,
  "rationale": "Time of day is a generally stable driver of taxi demand, traffic, and trip patterns, so it should remain broadly predictive across domains; however, exact minute can also capture city-specific schedules, nightlife/commute artifacts, and shift-dependent patterns that may vary across environments, so it is useful but not maximally robust."
}

{
  "task_summary": "Predict whether a taxi trip outcome of interest will occur from trip metadata in a way that generalizes across distribution shifts.",
  "feature_id": 6,
  "score": 0.42,
  "rationale": "Travel bearing can carry some route and geography signal related to trip outcomes, but it is often highly location- and city-pattern-dependent, interacts strongly with origin/destination, and may be brittle across temporal or regional domain shifts rather than a broadly stable standalone predictor."
}
